# Sample .gitignore for tracking / with etckeeper
#
# Place this file at /.gitignore, then run:
#   etckeeper init -d /
#
# Git ignores everything by default. Add the paths you want to track by
# un-ignoring them with "!" lines below. After init, use normal git commands
# (git add, git commit) to manage your tracked files.
#
# --- How negation rules work ---
# To un-ignore a nested path every component of that path must be un-ignored.
# Example: to track /home/alice/.bashrc you need three lines:
#   !/home
#   !/home/alice
#   !/home/alice/.bashrc
# Stopping at any level leaves everything deeper ignored.
#
# Un-ignoring a directory tracks its entire contents. To track only selected
# files inside a large directory, ignore its contents first, then carve out
# what you want:
#   !/etc
#   /etc/*
#   !/etc/fstab
#   !/etc/hosts
#
# After init, etckeeper appends its own "managed by etckeeper" block to this
# file. Your custom rules must appear above that block (put them here).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Ignore everything at the root level.
/*

# Un-ignore the paths you want to track. Some common examples:

# Track all of /etc (the classic etckeeper use case).
# !/etc

# Track a single file in a home directory.
# !/home
# !/home/username
# !/home/username/.bashrc

# Track boot configuration.
# !/boot
